MKFS(8)                        P32W Unix                         MKFS(8)

NAME
       mkfs - construct a file system

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/mkfs special nblocks [ninodes]

DESCRIPTION
       mkfs writes an empty filesystem of nblocks 1024-byte blocks
       onto the block device special, in the format of fs(5): a
       superblock, the inode table, the free-block bitmap and a
       root directory (inode 1, mode 755, owned by root) holding
       only . and .., in the first data block. The superblock is
       marked clean.

       ninodes is the number of inodes, by default one for every 8
       blocks; it is raised to at least 64, lowered to at most
       524288, and rounded up to a multiple of 16 (16 inodes fill a
       block). nblocks must be at least 64, and must leave room for
       the root directory after the inode table and the bitmap.

       When done, mkfs syncs and prints the layout, for example

              /dev/ram: 256 blocks, 64 inodes, data from block 6

       Mount the new filesystem with mount(8). Only root can write
       the block devices.

       mkfs refuses a device that is mounted (mkfs: special is
       mounted), the root filesystem included, and a size the
       device does not have: it first reads block nblocks-1, and if
       that fails says mkfs: special has fewer than nblocks blocks.
       Nothing is written in either case.

EXAMPLES
       /etc/mkfs /dev/ram 256        the whole RAM disk
       /etc/mkfs /dev/ram 256 128    the same with 128 inodes

EXIT STATUS
       0 on success; 1 if special is mounted, cannot be opened or
       written, or is smaller than nblocks; 2 for a usage error or
       a size that is too small.

FILES
       /dev/ram       the RAM disk: 256 blocks

SEE ALSO
       fs(5), mount(8), fsck(8), df(1)

BUGS
       A new disk image for the CompactFlash card, with the boot
       area and the kernel, is made on the host with
       tools/p32fs.py, not with mkfs.
